<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1556">1556</a>–<a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan (1556–1627) was a commander and poet at the Mughal court, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s guardian Bairam Khan. As one of Akbar's leading generals he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1584 and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, receiving the submission of the Tarkhan ruler Mirza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> in 1591. He translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s memoirs into Persian and is famous for his Hindi couplets (dohas) written under the name Rahim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-rahim-khan-i-khanan.jpg" alt="Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hashim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abd_al-Rahim,_Khan_Khanan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdul Rahim, popularly known as Rahim, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 17 December 1556, the son of Bairam Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s trusted guardian and mentor. After Bairam Khan was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Patan, Gujarat</a>, his first wife and the young Rahim were brought safely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and presented to Akbar, who gave him the title Mirza Khan and married him to Mah Banu, sister of Mirza Aziz Kokah. Akbar later married Bairam Khan's widow Salima Sultan Begum, Rahim's stepmother, which also made him Akbar's stepson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rahim became one of the nine important ministers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s court, known as the Navaratnas, and received the title Khan-i-Khanan, meaning Khan of Khans. He was also a general, sent to deal with the rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and later overall commander in the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. In 1580 Akbar made him chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and sent him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> of Mewar. Rahim left his family in Sherpura and advanced; Pratap's son Prince Amar Singh captured the women of Rahim's family, but Pratap rebuked him and ordered that they be returned with honour.</p><p>Rahim was known for giving alms with his eyes lowered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> sent him a couplet asking why he gave so, with hands held high and eyes low, and Rahim replied that the Giver was another, and that because people credited him, he kept his eyes down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rahim was known for his Hindustani dohe, or couplets, and for two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> books on astrology, Khetakautukam and Dwatrimshadyogavali. He translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s memoirs, the Baburnama, from Chagatai into Persian, completing it in 1589–90. He died on 1 October 1627. His tomb in Nizamuddin East, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayuns-tomb">Humayun's Tomb</a>, was built by him for his wife in 1598, and his body was placed in it in 1627. In 1753–54 marble and sandstone from it were used to build Safdarjung's Tomb. The Aga Khan Trust for Culture restored it over six years and opened it to the public in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He escaped in 1583 and with the nobles' help briefly regained the throne before Akbar's general <b>Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</b> defeated him in January 1584.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mirza Jani Beg Tarkhan was defeated by Akbar's general <b>Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</b> and submitted in 1591.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarkhan-dynasty">Tarkhan dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1602" aria-label="Lahore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6468 1015 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6918 1285h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1602">What was here in 1602? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
